From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:08:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 13/22] Metal gridding: split-tile dispatch (the real-data win)

The 16x gridding/degridding gap on clustered data is not atomic contention. Two
contention hypotheses were measured and both failed: fewer threads per tile is
WORSE (lpc=8: 256 threads 14.5 ms, 128 23.7, 64 39.7, 32 63.5), and removing
the atomics entirely (the pull variant) is worse still, because one owner per
cell has to scan the whole tile bucket.

The limit is tile-level parallelism: clustered uv coverage puts all 440 745 rows
in a handful of tiles, so one threadgroup per tile runs a few thousand threads
on a GPU that wants ~128 000 - latency bound with the machine idle.

Fix: split each tile's bucket into slices of about ``split_target`` visibilities
and dispatch one threadgroup per slice, with a flat (tile, begin, end) work list
built on the host. Each slice keeps its own threadgroup tile and merges once
with device atomics, so the merge stays negligible and parallelism scales with
the slice count.

Real data, 440 745 rows, min of 7 within each of three runs:
  50.4 lambda/cell: 1 threadgroup/tile 28.5-33.9 ms -> split 256 9.2-13.1 ms
  8 lambda/cell:    14.0-22.9 ms -> 6.8-11.2 ms
With the 256 default the real-data round trip is 15.9 ms (50.4) and 13.2 ms
(8 lambda/cell) against ducc0's CPU 67.3 / 65.8 ms: 4.2-5.0x, up from 1.7-3.6x.
Accuracy is unchanged (same rel_max, adjoint 2-4e-6); uniform synthetic data is
unaffected because its slices are one per tile anyway.

Adds --tg and --split to the probe, and makes the poly kernels' threadgroup size
dynamic so both knobs are measurable.

